{{The geological history of the Grand Canyon is a remarkable record of Earth's past, spanning nearly two billion years. The story begins with the oldest exposed rocks, the Vishnu Schist, which formed approximately 1.8 billion years ago during the Precambrian era. These rocks are primarily metamorphic, indicating intense heat and pressure over time. Above the Vishnu Schist lies a series of ancient igneous intrusions known as the Zoroaster Granite, which formed as magma cooled and solidified. Together, these formations represent the foundation of the Grand Canyon's deep geological history, offering insights into ancient tectonic activity and the early continental crust.}}

{{Following the Precambrian, the Paleozoic era brought significant sedimentary rock layers, which are prominently displayed in the canyon's walls. Over 300 million years, shallow seas, coastal environments, and deserts deposited layers of limestone, sandstone, and shale. Notable formations from this era include the Muav Limestone, Bright Angel Shale, and Tapeats Sandstone. These layers contain fossils of marine life, such as trilobites and brachiopods, painting a vivid picture of ancient ocean environments. The remarkable sequence of rock formations records the dynamic shifts in sea level and climate, aiding geologists in reconstructing past ecosystems and the environments in which prehistoric organisms thrived.}}

{{The Mesozoic and Cenozoic eras saw further transformation, although many of these rock layers have since eroded away. The rise of the Rocky Mountains and volcanic activity significantly influenced the region's topography. The Grand Canyon as we see it today began to form around five to six million years ago, as the Colorado River carved through these rock layers, driven by tectonic uplift and the river's erosive power. This ongoing geological process continues to shape the canyon, offering a living laboratory for understanding Earth's geological processes. The Grand Canyon not only serves as a testament to the planet’s dynamic history but also provides an invaluable window into the environments where ancient life once flourished.}}

